  {:slug "package-scoped-instrument-dependency-identity",
   :title "Scope Instrument Dependency Identity to the Package, Not the Workspace",
   :status :accepted,
   :date "2026-07-26",
   :accepted "2026-07-26",
   :validation-scope :structural,
   :release-authority :development,
   :topics [:parser :release],
   :relations
   [{:class :lifecycle,
     :type :amends,
     :to "parser-release-instrument-bindings",
     :scope "instrument semantic identity and its dependency boundary"}],
   :claims
   [{:id :c1,
     :kind :structural-invariant,
     :statement
     "The `parser-ir-conformance` instrument binds a package-scoped locked dependency projection — the transitive `Cargo.lock` closure of `ab-aat-to-parser-ir` alone, as name, version, and checksum — rather than the workspace lockfile's bytes, and reads `jsonschema_crate` out of that projection instead of restating it as a literal. Adding a workspace package the instrument does not depend on no longer rotates its identity; a version change inside its own closure still does. The closure is keyed by name and version, because thirty-six names resolve to several versions at once and collapsing them by name both substitutes one version's entry for another's and drops the siblings; a dependency entry that names no single locked package is rejected rather than guessed at. Two gaps are stated rather than closed: workspace-local members carry no checksum and are marked `\"origin\": \"workspace-local\"` because their bytes lie outside this identity, and `Cargo.lock` records no feature selection. Neither gap was closed by hashing the lockfile whole. The projection's discrimination is checked in the `ab-validator` flake check `parser-rq-publication-pytest`, outside the evidence roots; within them the governed consequence is that the committed policies equal what the generator produces, so the capture re-derives from them. Evidence boundary: `test/abc/tools/parser_rq_predicate_hardening_capture_test.clj`.",
     :evidence
     ["abc/test/abc/tools/parser_rq_predicate_hardening_capture_test.clj"]}
    {:id :c2,
     :kind :structural-invariant,
     :statement
     "The `diagnostic-completeness` reviewed closure is the seven sources that survive `abc/src/abc/tools/evidence_io.clj`'s deletion under ADR 0043, which also removed the three sources reachable only through it. Its previous identity covered bytes absent from the repository and its generator refused to run. Both instruments' identities therefore rotate together, and the manifest schema is `parser-rq-predicate-validator-identity-v2`. Evidence boundary: `test/abc/tools/parser_rq_predicate_hardening_capture_test.clj`.",
     :evidence
     ["abc/test/abc/tools/parser_rq_predicate_hardening_capture_test.clj"]}
    {:id :c3,
     :kind :fixture-behavior,
     :statement
     "The rotation changes no measured value. Re-deriving the committed predicate-hardening capture from the live policies reproduces every record byte-for-byte, and regenerating the fixture moved only content addresses: each blob is identical once `policy_hash` is projected out, and the two index blobs differ solely in the refs their re-addressed records produced. The committed policies remain schema-valid and closed under the rotated hashes. Evidence boundary: `test/abc/tools/parser_rq_diagnostic_completeness_test.clj` and `test/abc/tools/parser_rq_parser_ir_conformance_test.clj`.",
     :evidence
     ["abc/test/abc/tools/parser_rq_diagnostic_completeness_test.clj"
      "abc/test/abc/tools/parser_rq_parser_ir_conformance_test.clj"]}]}
  {:slug "governed-diagnostic-expectation-and-instrument-coordinate",
   :title
   "Bind Diagnostic Expectation to the Corpus, Instrument Identity to Its Own Coordinate",
   :status :accepted,
   :date "2026-07-26",
   :accepted "2026-07-26",
   :validation-scope :structural,
   :release-authority :development,
   :topics [:parser :release],
   :relations
   [{:class :lifecycle,
     :type :amends,
     :to "parser-release-instrument-bindings",
     :scope "diagnostic expectation authority and the instrument identity coordinate"}
    {:class :lifecycle,
     :type :depends-on,
     :to "package-scoped-instrument-dependency-identity"}],
   :claims
   [{:id :c1,
     :kind :structural-invariant,
     :statement
     "Observed diagnostics are compared against each corpus entry's `:expected_diagnostics`, and that field joins `corpus-entry-identity-keys`. A global \"at least one diagnostic\" floor is rejected: it is satisfiable by any single diagnostic anywhere, says nothing about which works produced which, and would fail a corpus governed to be clean. The accepted verdict recorded `diagnostic_completeness 1.0` with `diagnostic_count 0` and `vacuous true` — consistent with a corpus declaring `:expected_diagnostics []` on every entry, but that declaration was read by nothing and bound into no identity. The instrument now derives `diagnostic_completeness` as works matching their governed expectation over expected works, a work whose expectation the policy does not restate cannot be measured at all, and the campaign orchestrator authenticates the policy's restatement against the governed corpus before any capture runs. Evidence boundary: `test/abc/tools/parser_rq_diagnostic_completeness_test.clj`.",
     :evidence
     ["abc/test/abc/tools/parser_rq_diagnostic_completeness_test.clj"]}
    {:id :c2,
     :kind :structural-invariant,
     :statement
     "A new `instrument_policy_hashes` key in the qualification identity binds each instrument's governed `policy_hash`, replacing reliance on the predicate set's descriptive `:instrument` prose. Editing that prose is rejected as the remedy because it rotates `predicate_set_hash`, braiding what must be true with how it was measured. The candidate schema declares `identity` with `additionalProperties: false`, so the key is a schema change and `parser-rq-candidate.schema.json` bumps off `schema_version` `1.0.0`. What the key binds is the content of each governed authority document — the sha256 of its canonical JSON bytes — not the `policy_hash` the document declares about itself: the ignored-regions taxonomy behind source recognition declares no hash at all, so a declaration-based rule would have had to omit that member and reintroduce the gap one level down, and where a policy does declare one that declaration is among the hashed bytes. Membership is closed over exactly the seven capture members that contribute observations, and a named-but-missing policy fails the candidate build. Evidence boundary: `test/abc/tools/parser_rq_campaign_test.clj`.",
     :evidence
     ["abc/test/abc/tools/parser_rq_campaign_test.clj"]}
    {:id :c3,
     :kind :structural-invariant,
     :statement
     "The predicate declares `:unit \"ratio\"` while `diagnostic_completeness` is a literal `1.0` and `complete_diagnostics` is assigned equal to `emitted_diagnostics` unconditionally, so no ratio is computed. The declaration is made true rather than corrected: `diagnostic_completeness` becomes the works whose observed diagnostics match their governed expectation over expected works. On the present corpus that is 3/3 = 1.0 and no outcome changes. Editing the declaration instead would rotate `predicate_set_hash`, braiding the predicate contract with an instrument concern — the same fault c2 rejects — so the dimension, comparator, threshold, and unit all stay as written and the semantics change is carried by a bumped `algorithm_version` in the instrument policy. The ratio is computed as IEEE division of two doubles rather than `(double (/ int int))`, whose exact-Ratio rounding differs in the last bit from every other language's division and so would have disagreed with the Python capture driver over a value that lands in content-addressed evidence. Evidence boundary: `test/abc/tools/parser_rq_diagnostic_completeness_test.clj`.",
     :evidence
     ["abc/test/abc/tools/parser_rq_diagnostic_completeness_test.clj"]}]}
  {:date "2026-07-27",
   :slug "parser-rq-instrument-before-threshold",
   :release-authority :development,
   :relations
   [{:class :lifecycle,
     :type :depends-on,
     :to "parser-release-instrument-bindings"}
    {:class :lifecycle,
     :type :depends-on,
     :to "governed-qualification-corpus-location"}],
   :claims
   [{:id :c1,
     :kind :corpus-behavior,
     :statement
     "The `source-span-coverage := 1.0` predicate fails on 299 of 299 sampled real works (fold 0.9640, worst 0.2466) while the governed three-work corpus returns exactly 1.0 from the same built `ab-parser-rq-source-accountability` binary, same taxonomy and same policy hash. A 177-byte corpus is why the divergence was invisible: it is small enough that the instrument's defect does not show. Measurement over a 299-work sample under a synthesized qualification identity; it establishes behaviour, not a qualified campaign."}
    {:id :c2,
     :kind :structural-invariant,
     :statement
     "The predicate divides a body-derived numerator by a whole-file denominator. The classified-source ledger is built by lexing `decoded.span_text`, which is the `aozora_body_range` body projection, while the recognition instrument takes `eligible_bytes` from the whole decoded file. Aozora's header and `底本：` colophon are therefore not unclassified — they lie outside the coordinate space every classified-source fact comes from, and no policy rule can reach them. Demonstrated on a synthetic work: three entries covering 51 of 375 bytes, with the entire 216-byte header and 108-byte tail carrying no entry of any kind, not even `plain_text`. **Measured 2026-07-27 through the built instrument, that demonstration is true only of LF sources and the shortfall is not entirely this.** Real Aozora sources are CRLF, and `sanitizer_entries` walks the whole sanitized text rather than the body, so every line ending in the header and tail is already a `crlf_normalization` fact. On three real works totalling 850,980 decoded bytes the whole-file fold is 0.9869 and the body-projection fold is 0.9911: the header and tail account for 3,624 of 11,139 gap bytes, and 7,515 -- two thirds -- lie inside the body. The coordinate mismatch is real and is worth fixing on its own terms, but it is not the whole shortfall, and a body denominator does not approach 1.0. Evidence boundary: `test/abc/tools/parser_rq_source_accountability_test.clj`. Two earlier statements of this mechanism — a missing `publication_metadata` rule, then a corrected byte split — inferred a cause from a correlation and were wrong; they are not preserved as claims. Mechanism claims in this area require a source-line trace."}
    {:id :c3,
     :kind :domain-interpretation,
     :statement
     "A predicate threshold may be relaxed only once its instrument is known to measure what it claims. Relaxing first converts an instrument defect into a permanent governed allowance, and a corpus small enough to hide the defect would have kept that allowance invisible indefinitely. So the instrument is fixed and neither the threshold nor the ratio moves on account of this gap."}
    {:id :c4,
     :kind :domain-interpretation,
     :statement
     "Redefining the ratio as `accounted / eligible` is rejected in kind, not merely as insufficient: `recovered_verbatim` means the parser could not type a construct, which is precisely what a recognition predicate should count against, so folding it into the numerator would make the predicate easier to pass by making it say less. Lowering the threshold is rejected for now and becomes admissible only after the instrument measures its declared contract, since some residual is genuine parser limitation that no instrument work will remove."}],
   :source
   "`docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-26-parser-rq-corpus-tiering-design.md`",
   :title "Fix the Instrument Before Setting the Residual Threshold",
   :topics [:parser :release],
   :status :proposed,
   :validation-scope :structural}
  {:date "2026-07-27",
   :slug "parser-rq-source-region-partition",
   :release-authority :development,
   :relations
   [{:class :lifecycle,
     :type :depends-on,
     :to "parser-rq-instrument-before-threshold"}
    {:class :lifecycle,
     :type :depends-on,
     :to "custom-parser-release-qualification"}],
   :claims
   [{:id :c1,
     :kind :structural-invariant,
     :statement
     "The decoded source is partitioned into exactly three declared regions derived solely from `aozora_body_range`: header `[0, body_start)`, body `[body_start, body_end)`, and tail `[body_end, len)`. The body carries the work — prose and annotations. The header and tail carry metadata about the work. No separator or `底本：` heuristic is maintained anywhere else; an earlier hand-rolled detector scanning for a 40-hyphen substring matched sixteen overlapping positions inside a single 55-hyphen rule, and 23 of 299 sampled works lack two separator lines entirely. The three regions are pairwise disjoint and their union is the whole decoded file, with no fourth region and no unassigned byte. The instrument asserts that conservation and fails closed when it does not hold. Evidence attached 2026-07-31, once cross-tree paths became citable: `the_published_regions_partition_the_decoded_source` pins conservation from the published record alone, and `malformed_region_sets_are_rejected_rather_than_arithmetic_faults` pins the fail-closed path.",
     :evidence
     ["ab-validator/crates/ab-parser-rq-source-accountability/tests/regions.rs"
      "ab-validator/crates/ab-parser-rq-source-accountability/tests/recognition_corpus.rs"]}
    {:id :c2,
     :kind :domain-interpretation,
     :statement
     "The body and the packaging metadata are two populations, not two candidate denominators for one ratio. A single ratio over their union averages parser fidelity against packaging attribution and can mean neither: the observed 0.9640 could not distinguish \"the parser missed prose\" from \"packaging is never lexed\", and establishing that it was entirely the latter took three attempts. `source_span_coverage` is therefore body-projection coverage — classified-source ledger facts over `decoded.span_text`, divided by the body projection — with numerator and denominator in one declared coordinate. It is not a whole-file measure and must not be read as one. Evidence attached 2026-07-31: `body_intervals_never_escape_the_body_region` pins numerator containment, and `eligibility_is_the_body_and_the_two_populations_still_sum_to_the_file` pins that the two populations remain a partition rather than two readings of one ratio.",
     :evidence
     ["ab-validator/crates/ab-parser-rq-source-accountability/tests/regions.rs"]}
    {:id :c3,
     :kind :domain-interpretation,
     :statement
     "Packaging metadata is measured by a separate attribution predicate over the header and tail regions in their own coordinate. This is an attribution measure over packaging, not a parser-fidelity measure, and it does not widen the parser's contract beyond the parser's input. It requires a fact producer before it requires a policy rule: the declared-but-unimplemented `publication_metadata` role classifies nothing until facts for those bytes exist. **Implemented 2026-07-27, deliberately partially.** `metadata_entries` classifies exactly one metadata form, the `key：value` colophon line -- `底本：`, `入力：`, `校正：`, `初出：` -- under a `publication_metadata_line / publication_metadata / structural_control / structural_token` rule, and leaves everything else in the header and tail unattributed. Blanket-claiming every metadata byte would drive the measure to 1.0 by construction, and a predicate that cannot fail measures nothing; the editorial legend block and the separator rules need their own classification before they can be counted. Measured on three real works, metadata attribution moved 0.0508 to 0.3002 while body coverage stayed at 0.9911, which is the check that the producer stayed inside its own population. Roughly 70% of packaging bytes still have no classifier, and that is the reason the metadata threshold cannot be predeclared: a threshold fixed against this instrument would encode \"colophon fields are classified and nothing else is\" as a permanent governed allowance, which is the error Q14 rejected. **Also corrected 2026-07-27: those bytes were never entirely unlexed.** `sanitizer_entries` walks the whole sanitized text rather than the body, so on CRLF sources -- which real Aozora files are -- every header and tail line ending already carried a `crlf_normalization` fact with a `structural_newline` role: 97 such facts over three real works, 194 of 3,818 metadata bytes. The colophon-field producer therefore excludes line terminators, because two producers must not claim the same interval. Evidence boundary: `test/abc/tools/validate_design_bundle_test.clj`.",
     :evidence
     ["abc/test/abc/tools/validate_design_bundle_test.clj"
      "abc/test/fixtures/parser-rq/classified-source/ledger.json"
      "ab-validator/crates/ab-parser-rq-source-accountability/tests/regions.rs"]}
    {:id :c4,
     :kind :domain-interpretation,
     :statement
     "Header and tail are recorded as distinct regions so that a failure localizes to one end of the file, and they qualify under a single conjunctive metadata predicate rather than one predicate each. A work is cleared only when body-projection coverage and metadata attribution both clear; neither alone qualifies a work. This is not the denominator reduction Q11 rejected: no byte leaves the accounting, the metadata bytes move to a different accounted region, and the conservation identity is what keeps that honest."}
    {:id :c5,
     :kind :domain-interpretation,
     :statement
     "Neither threshold is fixed before its instrument has been built and measured under an explicitly non-authoritative exploratory campaign carrying its own predicate-set identity. `source_span_coverage`'s prior `:= 1.0` was declared against a whole-file denominator; under a body denominator the same literal is a different contract, so it is not carried forward by default. The ≈0.9889 estimate circulating for the post-fix value derives from a disposable separator heuristic rather than from `aozora_body_range` and is not an acceptance value. The confirmatory campaign takes a distinct predicate-set identity from the exploratory one. Partially discharged 2026-07-31: `source_span_coverage`'s `:= 1.0` was consciously redeclared against the body denominator after the corpus-wide re-verification, with the v4 instrument rotation, and `predicate-set-binds-the-committed-instrument-with-a-redeclared-threshold` pins that the committed predicate set carries the redeclaration rather than an inheritance; the metadata threshold remains unpredeclared exactly as this claim requires.",
     :evidence
     ["abc/test/abc/tools/parser_rq_source_accountability_test.clj"]}
    {:id :c6,
     :kind :structural-invariant,
     :statement
     "`aozora_body_range`'s own return value does not partition the file, so the region derivation does not use it verbatim. It returns `(body_start..body_end, tail_start)` where `body_end` is trim-adjusted for trailing newlines while `tail_start` is not, so whenever a tail exists at least one byte lies between them. **The tail region is `[body_end, len)`, not `[tail_start, len)`** — it extends backward to meet the body, absorbing the blank lines that separate them. That is symmetric with the header, which already absorbs its adjacent blank lines because `skip_blank_lines` advances `body_start` past them. Widening `body_end` forward to `tail_start` is rejected as the alternative: it places transcriber whitespace into the body population, which would make body coverage depend on how many blank lines a transcriber left, whereas metadata attribution over packaging whitespace is trivially satisfiable — the ledger vocabulary already carries a `newline` disposition. Changing `aozora_body_range` itself is also rejected: `sanitized.body` is the projection handed to the parser, so moving that boundary would move `source_span_coverage`'s numerator as a side effect of a partition fix. Works with no `底本：` line (`body_end` and `tail_start` both equal `len`, so the tail is empty), with fewer than two separator lines, and with bare-CR line endings are boundary cases of the same derivation and are covered by tests. Evidence attached 2026-07-31: `the_published_regions_partition_the_decoded_source` asserts the tail slice includes the blank line the body end trimmed — the `[body_end, len)` derivation, not `[tail_start, len)`.",
     :evidence
     ["ab-validator/crates/ab-parser-rq-source-accountability/tests/regions.rs"]}],
   :source
   "`docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-27-q15-region-partition.md`",
   :title "Partition the Source into Body and Metadata Populations",
   :topics [:parser :release],
   :status :proposed,
   :validation-scope :structural}
  {:date "2026-07-27",
   :slug "parser-rq-classified-source-policy-binding",
   :release-authority :development,
   :relations
   [{:class :lifecycle,
     :type :amends,
     :to "parser-release-instrument-bindings",
     :scope "the source_recognition instrument policy closure"}
    {:class :lifecycle,
     :type :depends-on,
     :to "package-scoped-instrument-dependency-identity"}],
   :claims
   [{:id :c1,
     :kind :structural-invariant,
     :statement
     "`instrument_policy_hashes[:source_recognition]` binds the classified-source policy `data/parser-rq-ab-aozora-classified-source-v1.json` alongside the ignored-regions taxonomy it already bound. The policy's rules decide which eligible bytes are recognized and therefore decide `source_span_coverage`, while the parallel diagnostic-gap policy was already bound — so the documented authority closure did not match the instrument's actual authority. `instrument-policy-paths` widens from member to one path into member to an ordered vector of paths. The wire shape stays `member -> sha256`: a single-document member retains its document hash byte-for-byte, and a multi-document member folds its documents' hashes in declared order, so `parser-rq-candidate.schema.json` is untouched. Evidence boundary: `test/abc/tools/parser_rq_campaign_test.clj`.",
     :evidence ["abc/test/abc/tools/parser_rq_campaign_test.clj"]}
    {:id :c2,
     :kind :fixture-behavior,
     :statement
     "At the same commit and the same parser revision, editing the classified-source policy moves `instrument_policy_hashes[\"source_recognition\"]` and hence `qualification_identity_ref`, while leaving `predicate_set_hash` unchanged — the two rotation routes stay separate. Measured over the live tree, exactly one member coordinate moved. This rotates `qualification_identity_ref`, so captures made against the prior identity are stale; that is the intended consequence, and it is what makes a later recognition-policy amendment attributable. Evidence boundary: `test/abc/tools/parser_rq_campaign_test.clj`.",
     :evidence ["abc/test/abc/tools/parser_rq_campaign_test.clj"]}
    {:id :c3,
     :kind :domain-interpretation,
     :statement
     "This corrects an attribution defect, not silent drift, and the distinction decides what the fix has to be. A policy edit could not pass unnoticed before the binding: `parser-rq-classified-source-authority-v1.json` pins the policy by both `raw_bytes_hash` over its exact file bytes and `identity_hash` over its canonical JSON, and fails closed when either moves; `parser_git_rev` is a field of the identity; and the capture binary embeds the policy with `include_bytes!` so its sha256 moves too. What the identity could not do was *name* what changed — it rotated on every commit for any reason, so a recognition-policy amendment was indistinguishable from an unrelated one. A detection fix would therefore have been redundant; an attribution fix was not. The bound value is the policy's canonical bytes rather than its authority document, so the pin and the content stay one coordinate. Evidence boundary: `test/abc/tools/validate_design_bundle_test.clj`.",
     :evidence ["abc/test/abc/tools/validate_design_bundle_test.clj"]}],
   :source
   "`docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-26-parser-rq-corpus-tiering-design.md`",
   :title "Bind the Classified-Source Policy into the Qualification Identity",
   :topics [:parser :release :identity],
   :status :accepted,
   :accepted "2026-07-31",
   :validation-scope :structural}
  {:date "2026-07-27",
   :slug "parser-rq-retire-node-span-coverage",
   :release-authority :development,
   :relations
   [{:class :lifecycle,
     :type :depends-on,
     :to "parser-release-instrument-bindings"}],
   :claims
   [{:id :c1,
     :kind :corpus-behavior,
     :statement
     "`:parser_ir_node_span_coverage` and the `parser_ir.nodes[*].span` coverage basis do not measure source coverage. Measured through the built `ab-parser-rq-source-accountability` binary: the three governed works of 43, 58 and 76 source bytes all report `covered_eligible_bytes` of exactly 25, because all three emit the same eight visible characters plus a newline — the numerator does not move with the source. On one real work, slicing the decoded source at each node's span reproduces that node's own text 7 times in 8,137. The quantity is approximately visible-text bytes divided by decoded-source bytes: a markup-density ratio published as coverage. Every record measured returned `status` \"ok\" with no errors."}
    {:id :c2,
     :kind :structural-invariant,
     :statement
     "The cause is one coordinate name meaning two things in two crates. `map_node_span` (`ab-aat-to-parser-ir/src/convert.rs`) discards the AAT span's byte offsets, emits the caller's running accumulator over each node's own emitted text, and hard-codes `coordinate_system` to `decoded_utf8`; `sentences.rs` uses that same string for the visible-text projection and states the invariant that depends on it. `analyze.rs` reads it as the decoded source file. The guard cannot fire, because it compares the label against a value the emitter hard-codes, and bounds validation cannot catch it either: emitted text is shorter than its source, so every interval validates. `Interval::new` checks only `start <= end <= bound` and, unlike the recognition path, never checks `is_char_boundary` — a span ending mid-character in the decoded file is accepted silently."}
    {:id :c3,
     :kind :structural-invariant,
     :statement
     "Every consumer of the coverage quantity is a test. `install-source-recognition-observation`, which demotes the former observation to `:parser_ir_node_span_coverage`, has one caller and it is `test/abc/tools/parser_release_qualification_test.clj`. `derive-source-span-envelope`, which turns the P1 aggregate's `covered_eligible_bytes` into a ratio, has nine references and all are in `test/abc/tools/parser_rq_source_accountability_test.clj`. The field appears nowhere in `docs/reports/parser-release-qualification-measurements.edn`. The release-authoritative envelope is `derive-source-recognition-envelope`, which reads the recognition index and aggregate, not the node-span aggregate. No predicate declares the key. Confirmed by execution rather than by search alone: deleting all of it broke nothing outside the deleted set. Evidence boundary: `test/abc/tools/parser_rq_source_accountability_test.clj`.",
     :evidence
     ["abc/test/abc/tools/parser_rq_source_accountability_test.clj"]}
    {:id :c4,
     :kind :domain-interpretation,
     :statement
     "Retire the quantity: the `:parser_ir_node_span_coverage` measurement key, the `NodeSpans` coverage basis, the span-union computation, and the coverage byte fields it publishes. Renaming is rejected because a renamed field is still computed by unioning intervals from two coordinate systems and the published record still declares a coordinate it does not inhabit. Re-deriving it over genuine source offsets is rejected on its own motivation: the emitter would have to reconcile two coordinates rather than correct one, it is a parser-IR schema change with publication-pipeline blast radius, and no predicate wants the result. If a real parser-IR source-coverage measure is ever needed, a predicate that requires it should justify it. Evidence attached 2026-07-31, once cross-tree paths became citable: `node_spans_no_longer_affect_the_record_at_all` and `a_v1_record_cannot_be_read_as_v2` pin that v2 draws neither measurement nor verdict from the spans, and `the_retired_aggregate_subcommand_is_gone` pins the CLI surface.",
     :evidence
     ["ab-validator/crates/ab-parser-rq-source-accountability/tests/analyze_work.rs"
      "ab-validator/crates/ab-parser-rq-source-accountability/tests/corpus_index.rs"]}
    {:id :c5,
     :kind :structural-invariant,
     :statement
     "The retirement is scoped to the quantity, not to the analyzer. `analyze_corpus` also produces the membership index that `analyze_recognition_corpus` authenticates against, and the release-authoritative recognition path depends on it — for the closed work list, the identity reference, the coherence counts, and `membership_ref`, the hash of the index bytes recorded into every recognition record. It reads no coverage number from it. The per-work authentication that the index carries — parser-IR `schema_id` and `schema_hash` against the qualification identity, and `derived_from` against its AAT and mapping coordinates — is also retained. What is removed is the span union and the coverage fields -- and, decided 2026-07-27, the record's `eligible`/`ignored` fields and the P1 aggregate with them, reducing P1 to a provenance-and-authentication record so that the region partition does not inherit a whole-file eligibility it would have to undo. What is not removed is the membership derivation or its authentication. Evidence attached 2026-07-31: `a_real_membership_index_authenticates_recognition_and_seals_its_own_bytes` and `membership_bytes_are_rejected_when_reserialized_rather_than_replayed` pin the retained seam, and `the_membership_index_this_seam_rests_on_is_canonical_and_coverage_free` pins that the index carries no coverage.",
     :evidence
     ["ab-validator/crates/ab-parser-rq-source-accountability/tests/membership_seam.rs"
      "ab-validator/crates/ab-parser-rq-source-accountability/tests/corpus_index.rs"]}
    {:id :c6,
     :kind :structural-invariant,
     :statement
     "Consequences that must be carried rather than discovered. Changing what the membership index contains rotates `membership_ref` and therefore every recognition record, so this is a versioned instrument change and prior captured evidence becomes protocol-incompatible rather than merely stale. Re-measured 2026-07-27 on the governed corpus under the promoted qualification identity: `membership_ref` moved from `sha256:746b9989...6d2e78` to `sha256:93e9dbd4...39c9f18`, the per-work record went 1524 to 1225 bytes, `qualification_identity_ref` did not move, and recognition still returned `ok` on all three works across the rotation. The wire enums are closed with `deny_unknown_fields`, so no reinterpretation path exists in either direction, only re-capture. Eighteen published artifacts under `docs/reports/parser-rq/runs/` carry a v1 P1 wire version -- 9 work records, 3 aggregates and 6 indexes, exactly 6 per run across three runs, one of them the promoted run -- and the 9 work records are the subset carrying the `parser_ir.nodes[*].span` basis. An earlier draft of this claim said twenty-two artifacts carried the basis; that figure was unmeasured and wrong on both the count and the population. Published manifests are immutable, so none are rewritten: the v1 schemas are retained frozen alongside v2 as standalone copies rather than aliases, and a test validates every published v1 artifact against them and asserts the 9/3/6 counts so that a vanished artifact fails rather than shrinking the validated set. The 9.80 ms per work cost estimate was not re-measured and is therefore not claimed as a saving; the governed corpus is too small for the figure to mean anything and a real measurement requires a regenerated parser-IR against the pinned corpus. Evidence boundary: `test/abc/tools/parser_rq_source_accountability_test.clj`.",
     :evidence
     ["abc/test/abc/tools/parser_rq_source_accountability_test.clj"
      "abc/test/fixtures/parser-rq/parser-rq-source-accountability-work-v1.schema.json"
      "abc/test/fixtures/parser-rq/source-accountability/index.json"
      "ab-validator/crates/ab-parser-rq-source-accountability/tests/fixture_capture.rs"]}
    {:id :c7,
     :kind :domain-interpretation,
     :statement
     "One finding is deliberately not resolved here because it belongs to the parser-IR emitter rather than to this instrument: a single parser-IR document carries spans in two coordinate systems, both labelled `decoded_utf8`. Nodes run contiguously in accumulator coordinates while the `底本` `source-note` node carries a genuine decoded-source span that slices to exactly the colophon. `sentences.rs` describes this as a historical defect; it survives in the source-note arm. Retiring the coverage quantity removes the consumer that silently unioned across both, but it does not make the emitter's coordinate determinate, and that question needs its own owner."}],
   :source
   "`docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-27-q13-node-span-coverage.md`",
   :title "Retire Parser-IR Node-Span Coverage",
   :topics [:parser :release],
   :status :proposed,
   :validation-scope :structural}
  {:date "2026-07-27",
   :slug "parser-rq-publication-workload-snapshot",
   :release-authority :development,
   :relations
   [{:class :lifecycle,
     :type :depends-on,
     :to "custom-parser-release-qualification"}
    {:class :lifecycle,
     :type :depends-on,
     :to "sole-publication-release-identity"}],
   :claims
   [{:id :c1,
     :kind :domain-interpretation,
     :statement
     "Tier 2 of the parser-RQ corpus architecture binds to publication's successfully-selected source projection and is named the publication-workload snapshot. The decision was taken on constructibility rather than preference: a corpus entry's identity is `corpus-entry-identity-keys` — `:work_id :source_path :source_sha256 :category :expected_status :expected_diagnostics` — and `corpus-snapshot-hash` is the sha256 over every entry's sorted `:source_sha256`, while the pre-attempt candidate population carries only `{:file :relpath :row}`: no `:source_sha256`, no primary-text member, no content-addressed identity. Every missing field is produced by `source-bundle/inspect-zip` inside `inspect-selected-work!`, which is precisely the fallible step `select-candidate` wraps — a derivation-failed member has no source hash because it failed, and it cannot be hashed into `corpus-snapshot-hash` without inventing an absent-hash representation that weakens the pinning for every other entry. The successful projection is already content-addressed exactly as required: `source-selection-identity-object` is built from `selected` alone, from official Git/catalog/archive/bundle/primary-text/metadata facts and never from parser output."}
    {:id :c2,
     :kind :domain-interpretation,
     :statement
     "The denominator-shrinkage objection is answered by attestation, not by the downstream release gate. Binding to `selected` alone would leave a qualification report green on a corpus a `continue_on_failure` regression had quietly shrunk, so the tier-2 report must carry its own denominator: `candidates_considered` (the catalog-backed pre-attempt count), the `derive_failures` set and count, and the `rejected` set with reasons — all already computed and emitted by `materialize-selected-sources!`, so this is a projection of data publication already produces rather than new machinery. Extended 2026-07-31 with a fourth class for capture-time losses: works that decode-refuse at capture produce no record at all, and the recognition instrument's closed membership turns a missing record into an `:unavailable` observation rather than a smaller ratio, so a no-record work is either carried as `:unavailable` or excluded by a governed corpus-entry decision that rotates `corpus_list_hash`; the report lists them as `capture_refusals` with reasons. See the tiering design, *No-record works, accounted 2026-07-31*."}
    {:id :c3,
     :kind :domain-interpretation,
     :statement
     "Scope of the claim once Q9 proves population equality: a census of one snapshot coinciding with an operational claim about the publication workload — never a statistical claim about future Aozora works and never a language-conformance claim. The census expires when `aozorabunko-src` is bumped. The equality half of Q9 reduces to authenticating that the corpus was projected from `source_selection_hash` rather than re-derived, which is near-tautological by construction; the substantive Q9 work is the accounted set difference against the source-bundle admission universe and the conversion-audit population, where the 17,878 / 17,880 / 17,886 spread has to be explained. Q9 remains open, and no canonical join key is predeclared yet."}],
   :source
   "`docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-26-parser-rq-corpus-tiering-design.md`",
   :title "Bind Tier 2 to the Publication-Workload Snapshot",
   :topics [:parser :release :corpus],
   :status :proposed,
   :validation-scope :structural}
  {:date "2026-07-27",
   :slug "parser-rq-unexpected-fatal-failures",
   :release-authority :development,
   :relations
   [{:class :lifecycle,
     :type :depends-on,
     :to "custom-parser-release-qualification"}
    {:class :lifecycle,
     :type :depends-on,
     :to "parser-release-instrument-bindings"}],
   :claims
   [{:id :c1,
     :kind :structural-invariant,
     :statement
     "An adversarial fixture tier is incompatible with the current `fatal-failures <= 0` predicate. The core-attempt aggregate computes `:fatal_failures` by counting records whose disposition is `fatal_error`, with no reference to the corpus member's `:expected_status` — `allowed_dispositions` merely permits the value to travel through the capture protocol. A fixture that deliberately expects fatal rejection therefore increments the count and fails the predicate. `:expected_status` is in `corpus-entry-identity-keys`, so it already moves corpus identity, but no schema validates its vocabulary and nothing compares it against observed dispositions. Evidence boundary for the counting behaviour: `test/abc/tools/parser_rq_core_attempt_test.clj`.",
     :evidence ["abc/test/abc/tools/parser_rq_core_attempt_test.clj"]}
    {:id :c2,
     :kind :domain-interpretation,
     :statement
     "Decision (Option 1 of three): redefine the predicate as `unexpected-fatal-failures <= 0`, comparing each observed disposition against a governed expected outcome so that the predicate says what it means. This rotates instrument semantics and `predicate_set_hash`, hence `qualification_identity_ref`; it is sequenced with the wall-time reshape so one identity rotation covers both. The decision unblocks the work, not the tier: until the prerequisites land, the fixture tier still cannot carry adversarial cases. Prerequisites in order: (1) untangle `allowed_dispositions`, which lists the record-status value `protocol_error` inside a disposition vocabulary and conflates the schema's two levels; (2) specify the two-level expected-outcome model with closed, schema-enforced vocabularies including which values are never expectable; (3) adopt the safe initial contract; (4) map corpus expectations to wire values, authenticate exact membership between expectations and observed records, and specify behaviour for a missing or unknown expectation."}
    {:id :c3,
     :kind :domain-interpretation,
     :statement
     "The safe initial expected-outcome contract restricts expectations to `parsed` and `fatal_error`; `adapter_timeout`, `protocol_error`, and `unavailable` remain unconditional failures. An expected timeout would make the timeout predicate vacuous, and an expected protocol error would let a harness fault masquerade as a governed outcome."}],
   :source
   "`docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-26-parser-rq-corpus-tiering-design.md`",
   :title "Redefine Fatal Failures Against Governed Expected Outcomes",
   :topics [:parser :release],
   :status :proposed,
   :validation-scope :structural}]}
